## 4. Selecting Data

```python
df['column_name']                     # one column (a Series)
df[['col1', 'col2']]                  # multiple columns (note the double brackets)

df[df['col'] > 10]                    # filter: rows where col > 10
df[df['col'] == 'value']              # filter: rows where col equals 'value' (note ==, not =)

df[(df['col1'] > 5) & (df['col2'] == 'Yes')]   # AND — each condition in its own parentheses
df[(df['col1'] > 5) | (df['col2'] == 'Yes')]   # OR
```

---

## 5. Handling Missing Data

```python
df.isnull().sum()                          # count of missing values per column
df.dropna()                                # returns a new df with rows containing any missing value removed
df['col'] = df['col'].fillna(0)            # fill missing values with a specific value
df['col'] = df['col'].fillna(df['col'].mean())   # fill with the column's average
```

`np.nan` is how a missing value is represented when building data manually — it's NumPy's "not a number" marker, and it's what pandas itself uses internally to mark a gap.

---

## 6. Grouping & Aggregating

```python
df['col'].value_counts()                          # count of each unique value in one column

df.groupby('col')['other_col'].mean()              # average of other_col, per group
df.groupby('col')['other_col'].sum()                # total
df.groupby('col')['other_col'].count()              # count of non-null values per group
df.groupby('col')['other_col'].max()                 # max per group
df.groupby('col')['other_col'].min()                 # min per group

df.groupby('col')['other_col'].agg(['count', 'mean'])   # multiple aggregates at once

df.groupby('col1')['col2'].value_counts()            # counts of col2, broken out by col1 group
```

