## 8. Joining Two DataFrames

The pandas equivalent of SQL's `JOIN` is `.merge()`. The `how` parameter controls which join type it behaves like:

```python
pd.merge(df1, df2, on='shared_column', how='inner')
```

| `how` value | Behaves like | Keeps |
|---|---|---|
| `'inner'` | `INNER JOIN` | Only rows that match in both DataFrames |
| `'left'` | `LEFT JOIN` | All rows from `df1`, matched or not |
| `'right'` | `RIGHT JOIN` | All rows from `df2`, matched or not |
| `'outer'` | `FULL JOIN` | All rows from both, matched or not |

```python
# Example: keep every patient, even those with no lab results
merged_df = pd.merge(patients_df, lab_results_df, on='patient_id', how='left')
```

