## 9. Common Gotchas

- **`==` vs `=`** — Python uses `==` for "is equal to" in a condition. A single `=` assigns a value instead, and will throw an error or silently do the wrong thing if used in a filter.
- **Double brackets for multiple columns** — `df[['col1', 'col2']]` needs the outer bracket ("give me columns from df") and an inner list (`['col1', 'col2']`) of which ones.
- **Curly/smart quotes break code** — if text is copied from Word, Google Docs, iMessage, or similar, apostrophes and quotation marks can get auto-converted into "smart" curly versions (`’` instead of `'`). Python doesn't recognize these as valid string quotes and will throw a syntax error. Always retype quotes directly in Colab/your code editor, or paste into a plain-text area first to strip formatting.
- **Cell execution order in notebooks** — cells run in whatever order you click "Run" on them, not their order on the page. If `df` gets reassigned by an old cell run out of order, later work can silently disappear. Use **Runtime → Restart session** then **Runtime → Run all** to confirm a notebook runs cleanly top to bottom.
- **`.groupby()` output isn't always a DataFrame** — a single-column `.groupby(...)['col'].mean()` returns a Series, which prints differently than a full DataFrame. This is expected, not an error.
- **`(cond1) & (cond2)` needs parentheses around each condition** — leaving them off causes an error, unlike SQL's `AND`, which doesn't require this.
- **`pd.merge()` needs a shared column name** — if the key column has different names in each DataFrame, use `left_on='col_a', right_on='col_b'` instead of `on='col'`.

